add(slug="how-to-stop-doomscrolling", tag="Habits", mins=8,
 title="How to Stop Doomscrolling: 7 Tactics That Actually Work",
 seo="How to Stop Doomscrolling: 7 Tactics That Work | Brainrot",
 desc="Doomscrolling isn't a character flaw. Why feeds are so sticky, and 7 practical tactics, from video limits to bedtime rules, that break the habit.",
 keywords="how to stop doomscrolling, doomscrolling, stop scrolling on phone, break scrolling habit, doomscrolling app, stop reels addiction",
 related=["why-willpower-fails-against-algorithms","how-to-reduce-screen-time-android","dopamine-detox-guide"],
 lede="You open your phone to check one message. Forty minutes later you're still swiping, tired and oddly unable to press the lock button. That isn't a lack of discipline. It's a predictable response to how feeds are built.",
 body='''
<h2>Why doomscrolling is so hard to stop</h2>
<p>Short-video feeds use what psychologists call a <em>variable reward schedule</em>. Most videos are forgettable, but every so often one is funny, outrageous or fascinating. Because you can't predict which swipe will pay off, each swipe carries a little anticipation. B.F. Skinner's experiments showed that unpredictable rewards produce some of the most persistent behaviour, and slot machines use the same principle.</p>
<p>Feeds also remove natural stopping points. There's no end of the page, no credits, no "next episode?" prompt. Autoplay decides for you, and the next video is already loading before you've finished thinking about the last one.</p>
<blockquote>The feed doesn't need every video to be good. It only needs enough of them to keep your thumb moving.</blockquote>
<h2>Why most fixes fail</h2>
<p>The usual first attempt is a timer on the whole app: 30 minutes on Instagram, 30 minutes on YouTube. It tends to collapse within days, for a simple reason: you need those apps. You need Instagram to answer a friend and YouTube for a tutorial. When the timer fires, you tap "ignore limit" for a legitimate reason, and the feed is right there.</p>
<p>The fixes that last do two things: they target the specific behaviour, and they take the decision away from the moment when you're tired.</p>
<h2>Seven tactics that help</h2>
<h3>1. Limit videos, not apps</h3>
<p>Cap the feed itself and leave the rest of the app alone. A tool like <a href="../index.html">Brainrot</a> counts each Reel or Short and closes the feed at your limit, so messages and long videos never get locked out and you never need to override anything.</p>
<h3>2. Make the number visible</h3>
<p>Scrolling distorts your sense of time. A small live counter (for example "27 of 40") breaks the trance in a way a clock doesn't, because it measures what you're actually doing.</p>
<h3>3. Use one shared budget</h3>
<p>If you only cap Instagram, the habit moves to YouTube Shorts. A single daily budget across all feeds closes that loophole.</p>
<h3>4. Change the bedtime setup</h3>
<p>Most doomscrolling happens in bed. Charge your phone across the room, ideally with a cheap alarm clock doing the waking-up job. If you can't reach it, you can't scroll.</p>
<h3>5. Have a replacement ready</h3>
<p>You reach for the feed when you're bored, anxious or tired. Put something low-effort in its place: a paperback by the bed, a podcast, a short walk. A gap with nothing in it gets filled by the phone.</p>
<h3>6. Go greyscale during work hours</h3>
<p>Colour is part of what makes feeds rewarding. Android's greyscale option (Digital Wellbeing → Bedtime mode, or Developer options on some phones) makes the screen noticeably less appealing.</p>
<h3>7. Set the rule in the morning</h3>
<p>Choose your limit when you're clear-headed and let software enforce it. A decision you made at 8am is much easier to keep than one you're making at 11pm.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>A good starting point</strong>Check your current daily average for a couple of days, then set a limit about 25% below it. Lower it every week or two until it feels like a bit of a squeeze but not a punishment.</div>
<h2>What to expect</h2>
<p>The first few days feel boring, and that's the point. The urge to check usually peaks and fades within a couple of minutes if you let it. Most people find the urge weakens noticeably over two to three weeks of a consistent limit.</p>
<h2>When to get extra support</h2>
<p>If scrolling is seriously affecting sleep, work, relationships or mood, or you feel unable to cut back despite trying, speaking to a doctor or counsellor is a sensible step. Apps can help with habits, but they aren't a substitute for professional care.</p>''')

add(slug="how-to-reduce-screen-time-android", tag="How-to", mins=9,
 title="How to Reduce Screen Time on Android: A Practical Guide",
 seo="How to Reduce Screen Time on Android (2026 Guide)",
 desc="Reduce screen time on Android with built-in Digital Wellbeing tools, app limits, focus sessions and per-video Reels and Shorts limits. A setup that sticks.",
 keywords="how to reduce screen time android, reduce phone screen time, screen time limit android, digital wellbeing android, limit app usage android, android screen time tips",
 related=["best-app-blockers-android","how-to-stop-doomscrolling","instagram-reels-blocker-guide"],
 lede="Most advice about screen time is either vague (\"be more mindful\") or extreme (\"delete everything\"). Here is a practical sequence for Android, starting with tools you already have and ending with the part most of them can't do.",
 body='''
<h2>Step 1: Look at the real numbers</h2>
<p>Open <strong>Settings → Digital Wellbeing &amp; parental controls</strong>. The dashboard shows daily screen time, per-app usage and the number of times you unlocked your phone. Look at two or three days, not one. You are looking for the one or two apps that account for most of your time. That's where your effort should go.</p>
<p>Also note <em>when</em> the time happens. Late-night scrolling and idle-moment checking need different fixes.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Cut the triggers</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Turn off non-essential notifications.</strong> Most unlocks start with a buzz. Keep messages and calls, silence the rest.</li>
  <li><strong>Move the apps.</strong> Take the worst offenders off your home screen, or into a folder on the second page.</li>
  <li><strong>Use Bedtime mode.</strong> Digital Wellbeing's Bedtime mode can grey out the screen and silence notifications on a schedule.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Use built-in app timers, with realistic expectations</h2>
<p>Digital Wellbeing lets you set a daily timer on any app. When the time is up the app is paused for the rest of the day. This works well for apps you don't actually need, such as a game or a shopping app. It works poorly for apps that are part of your life, because you'll override it the first time you need to send a message.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>The override problem</strong>A timer that locks a whole app tends to get dismissed for good reasons, and a dismissed timer protects nothing. If an app has both a function you need and a feed you don't, you need a limit that can tell them apart.</div>
<h2>Step 4: Limit the feed, not the app</h2>
<p>The biggest time sinks on most phones are short-video feeds: Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts, Facebook Reels and Snapchat Spotlight. They live inside apps you use for other things. <a href="../index.html">Brainrot</a> counts each video and closes the feed at your daily limit, while DMs, Stories and long videos keep working. There's a fuller explanation on the <a href="../screen-time-controller.html">screen time controller page</a>.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Protect the hours that matter</h2>
<p>Schedule focus sessions for your best study or work hours. During a session, distracting apps are blocked and only the essentials you chose, like Phone and Messages, stay available. Set it up once and it runs on its own.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Review weekly</h2>
<p>Once a week, check your 7-day history. If you consistently hit your limit early, lower it a little. If you never come close, you can tighten it. The goal is a number that leaves you slightly bored, not deprived.</p>
<h2>A simple starter setup</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Setting</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Only messages and calls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bedtime mode</td><td>On, 10:30pm to 6:30am</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short-video limit</td><td>One shared limit, about 25% under your average</td></tr>
<tr><td>App timers</td><td>30 minutes on games, news and shopping apps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focus sessions</td><td>Your two best work or study windows</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Common questions</h2>
<h3>Does reducing screen time really matter?</h3>
<p>It depends on what the time is replacing. Time spent on calls with family or learning something is very different from passive scrolling you wouldn't choose on reflection. The aim isn't zero, it's spending time on purpose.</p>
<h3>What is a realistic target?</h3>
<p>Cutting your daily short-video time by a third to a half within a month is realistic for most people.</p>''')

add(slug="best-app-blockers-android", tag="Compare", mins=8,
 title="Best App Blockers for Android in 2026: How to Choose",
 seo="Best App Blockers for Android in 2026: How to Choose",
 desc="Not all Android app blockers solve the same problem. Compare full-app lockers, Digital Wellbeing, friction apps and per-video limiters, and pick what fits.",
 keywords="best app blocker android, android app blocker, best screen time app android, app blocker comparison, digital wellbeing alternative, social media blocker android",
 related=["how-to-reduce-screen-time-android","why-willpower-fails-against-algorithms","instagram-reels-blocker-guide"],
 lede="\"Best app blocker\" depends on what you are actually trying to block. A tool that is perfect for locking a game can be useless against the Reels tab. Here are the main types, what each does well, and who each is for.",
 body='''
<h2>The four types of Android blockers</h2>
<h3>1. Built-in tools (Digital Wellbeing)</h3>
<p>Free, preinstalled on most Android phones, and require no extra permissions. You get app timers, Focus mode and Bedtime mode. They are a solid first step. Their limit is that they work at the level of whole apps and total minutes.</p>
<h3>2. Full-app blockers</h3>
<p>These block selected apps entirely, often on a schedule or with a PIN. They're ideal for apps with no real utility during certain hours, such as a game during exams. They struggle with apps that mix a function you need with a feed you don't.</p>
<h3>3. Friction apps</h3>
<p>These add a pause, such as a breathing exercise or a question, before an app opens. They suit people whose problem is the automatic reach for the phone. They don't limit what happens once you're inside.</p>
<h3>4. Feed-level limiters</h3>
<p>These work inside an app. Brainrot is one: it counts Reels, Shorts and Spotlight videos and closes the feed at your limit, while messages and long videos stay usable. It is designed for people who need the app but not the feed.</p>
<h2>How to choose</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>If your problem is…</th><th>Look for…</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>An app you don't need at all (a game, a shopping app)</td><td>A full-app blocker or Digital Wellbeing timer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reaching for the phone out of habit</td><td>A friction app, or removing the app from the home screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reels or Shorts inside apps you also need</td><td>A feed-level limiter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not being able to focus on work or study</td><td>Scheduled focus sessions with an essentials list</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late-night scrolling</td><td>Bedtime mode plus a hard daily video limit</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>What to check before installing any blocker</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Permissions.</strong> Many blockers need Accessibility or Usage Access. That's normal, but make sure the app explains exactly what it does with it.</li>
<li><strong>Data.</strong> Does it need an account? Does it upload your usage? On-device processing is the safest design.</li>
<li><strong>Escape hatches.</strong> A blocker you can switch off in two taps won't hold at midnight. Look for options that add friction to disabling.</li>
<li><strong>Battery use.</strong> A reputable app shouldn't be near the top of your battery list.</li>
<li><strong>Essentials.</strong> Make sure calls and messages stay available.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Using more than one</h2>
<p>These tools aren't mutually exclusive. A common combination is Digital Wellbeing for Bedtime mode and notification control, plus a feed-level limiter for Reels and Shorts. See how they stack up on the <a href="../comparison.html">comparison page</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Disclosure</strong>We make Brainrot, so we are naturally biased toward feed-level limits. The type of tool that suits you depends on your habits. If you don't use short-video feeds, a different tool may serve you better.</div>''')

add(slug="dopamine-detox-guide", tag="Habits", mins=8,
 title="Dopamine Detox: A Realistic 7-Day Reset (Without the Hype)",
 seo="Dopamine Detox: A Realistic 7-Day Reset Plan | Brainrot",
 desc="\"Dopamine fasting\" is mostly a myth, but cutting back on hyper-stimulating feeds can help. A realistic 7-day reset for scrolling, focus and sleep.",
 keywords="dopamine detox, dopamine detox plan, 7 day dopamine detox, phone detox, reduce short form video, digital detox android",
 related=["how-to-stop-doomscrolling","short-form-video-addiction-effects","adhd-screen-time-management"],
 lede="\"Dopamine fasting\" went viral as a way to reset your brain. The science behind the name is shaky, but the practical idea, spending less time on hyper-stimulating feeds, is sound. Here's an honest version.",
 body='''
<h2>First, the myth</h2>
<p>You can't "detox" from dopamine. It is a neurotransmitter involved in motivation, movement and learning, and your brain produces it all the time. The original "dopamine fasting" idea, as popularised in tech circles, was actually a cognitive-behavioural technique for reducing impulsive behaviours, and it got garbled along the way.</p>
<p>What the research does support is more modest: constant, highly rewarding novelty makes slower activities feel flat by comparison, and reducing it for a while can make those activities feel more engaging again. That is a habit reset, not a chemical one.</p>
<h2>What you're actually doing</h2>
<p>A better name is a <strong>stimulation reset</strong>. You reduce the highest-intensity inputs (short-video feeds above all), keep the rest of life normal, and notice what changes in your attention, mood and sleep.</p>
<h2>The 7-day plan</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Day 1: Measure and cap.</strong> Check your current daily short-video count. Set a daily limit roughly half of it. Use one shared budget across Instagram, YouTube and the rest.</li>
<li><strong>Day 2: Remove the easy triggers.</strong> Turn off all non-essential notifications and move social apps off the home screen.</li>
<li><strong>Day 3: Protect your mornings.</strong> No phone for the first 30 to 45 minutes after waking. Use a physical alarm clock if you need one.</li>
<li><strong>Day 4: Single-task.</strong> Do one 50-minute block of focused work with the phone in another room. Notice how it feels at minute 10 and minute 40.</li>
<li><strong>Day 5: Replace, don't just remove.</strong> Pick something for the gaps: a walk, a book, a call with a friend.</li>
<li><strong>Day 6: Evening curfew.</strong> No screens for the last 45 to 60 minutes before bed.</li>
<li><strong>Day 7: A low-stimulation day.</strong> Keep short-video apps closed all day (set the limit to 0) and take stock.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>You don't have to be perfect</strong>If you slip on day 3, carry on with day 4. The plan is a structure for experimenting, not a test.</div>
<h2>What to track</h2>
<ul>
<li>Your daily short-video count (it should fall steadily)</li>
<li>How easy it is to start a hard task, rated 1 to 5 each evening</li>
<li>Sleep: when you fell asleep and how you felt in the morning</li>
</ul>
<h2>After the week</h2>
<p>Most people don't go back to zero. Instead, set a standing limit you can live with, perhaps 20 to 40 videos a day, and let the app enforce it. The reset is useful mostly because it shows you what a lower baseline feels like.</p>
<p class="disclaimer">This article is general information about habits, not medical advice. If you're struggling with your mental health, please speak to a healthcare professional.</p>''')

add(slug="short-form-video-addiction-effects", tag="Research", mins=7,
 title="What Short-Form Video Does to Your Attention (and What to Do About It)",
 seo="Short-Form Video and Attention: What the Research Says",
 desc="What do Reels and Shorts do to attention, memory and sleep? A plain-English look at what research suggests, what's still unclear, and how to respond.",
 keywords="short form video effects, reels effect on brain, short video addiction, shorts attention span, doomscrolling effects, social media attention span",
 related=["how-to-stop-doomscrolling","dopamine-detox-guide","why-willpower-fails-against-algorithms"],
 lede="Plenty of headlines claim short-form video is \"rotting\" your brain. The honest picture is more interesting: some effects are well supported, some are plausible but still being studied, and there's a lot you can do either way.",
 body='''
<h2>What the research reasonably supports</h2>
<h3>Interruptions are expensive</h3>
<p>Work by researchers such as Gloria Mark at UC Irvine has found that after an interruption, people often take a long time, sometimes over twenty minutes, to get fully back into a task. A feed makes many tiny interruptions easy, and each can cost far more than the seconds it takes.</p>
<h3>Heavy use is linked with weaker attention, though cause is unclear</h3>
<p>Several studies have found associations between heavy short-video use and lower scores on attention measures. Associations aren't proof of cause: people who already struggle with attention may be drawn to short videos. The direction of the effect is still being studied.</p>
<h3>Late-night use hurts sleep</h3>
<p>Evening screen use is repeatedly linked with later bedtimes and shorter sleep. Feeds make this worse because there's no natural stopping point.</p>
<h2>What's plausible but less certain</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Memory of what you watched.</strong> Rapid, unrelated clips are hard to hold onto. Many people notice they can't recall videos from ten minutes earlier.</li>
<li><strong>Boredom tolerance.</strong> If every spare moment is filled, the mind gets less unstructured time, which is when many people do their best background thinking.</li>
<li><strong>Preference for quick rewards.</strong> Slower activities can feel flat after a long session, though this is likely to be temporary and reversible.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What's probably exaggerated</h2>
<p>Claims that short videos permanently damage your brain, or that your attention span has collapsed to a specific number of seconds, aren't well supported. Attention is flexible, and habits can change.</p>
<h2>What to do</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Cap it.</strong> A daily video limit makes use deliberate rather than endless. See <a href="../how-it-works.html">how to set one up</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Protect your focused hours.</strong> Schedule focus sessions for your most important work.</li>
<li><strong>Make evenings screen-light.</strong> Stop short-video use at least 45 minutes before bed.</li>
<li><strong>Notice how you feel.</strong> The most reliable test is your own: how do you feel after 10 minutes versus after an hour?</li>
</ol>
<p class="disclaimer">This article is a general summary of research and is not medical advice. If you're worried about your attention, sleep or mental health, please speak to a doctor or qualified professional.</p>''')
